### **1. PwC 2026 AI Performance Study: 20% of Companies Capture 74% of AI Value**
*   **Published:** April 13, 2026
*   **Source:** PwC
*   **URL:** [https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/news-room/press-releases/2026/pwc-2026-ai-performance-study.html](https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/news-room/press-releases/2026/pwc-2026-ai-performance-study.html)
*   **Article Content:**
    LONDON, 13 April 2026 – A small group of companies is pulling sharply ahead in the race to generate real financial returns from artificial intelligence, according to PwC’s new AI Performance study. The global study interviewed 1,217 senior executives, primarily at large, publicly listed companies across 25 sectors, asking them about the revenue and efficiency gains they are seeing from AI today.

    It finds that nearly three‑quarters (74%) of AI’s economic value is captured by just one‑fifth (20%) of organisations, revealing a stark and widening divide between a small group of AI leaders and the majority of businesses still stuck in pilot mode. The research shows that these top‑performing companies are not simply deploying more AI tools. Instead, they are using AI as a catalyst for growth and business reinvention, particularly by pursuing new revenue opportunities created as industries converge, while building strong foundations around data, governance and trust.

    Key findings include:
    - **Growth vs. Productivity:** Leading companies are 2.6 times as likely to report AI improves their ability to reinvent their business model and 2 to 3 times as likely to use AI to identify growth opportunities from industry convergence.
    - **Decision Automation:** AI leaders are increasing the number of decisions made without human intervention at almost three times (2.8x) the rate of peers.
    - **Trust and Governance:** Leading firms are twice as likely to have employees who trust AI outputs, supported by Responsible AI frameworks (1.7x) and cross-functional governance boards (1.5x).
    
    “The leaders stand out because they point AI at growth, not just cost reduction,” said Joe Atkinson, Global Chief AI Officer at PwC. The study warns that without a shift in approach, the performance gap between AI leaders and laggards is likely to widen further as leaders scale proven use cases and automate decisions safely.

